# std::panic — stopping on purpose.
#
# `panic` is the first std function that promises never to come back. Its
# return type is `!`, the type `return` and `break` already had; writing it in
# a signature is what makes the promise checkable, and `!` standing in for any
# type is what makes `return panic(msg)` satisfy any return type. That is the
# whole reason `Option::unwrap` can exist.
#
# There is no unwinding and no `catch`: a panic sets the same error a failing
# native sets, so the VM reports it at the call site and stops. Recovering
# from one would need a story for what a half-finished frame leaves behind,
# and the language has none — so it does the honest thing and ends.

@native("panic_abort")
pub fun panic(message: string) -> !;
